{
  "id": "ep074",
  "duration": 52.92,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.995,
      "text": "Dead stars are supposed to destroy worlds, not build new ones.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of the WDJ0914+1914 system.tif",
        "credit": "ESO/M. Kornmesser",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_WDJ0914%2B1914_system.tif"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.0,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.995,
      "end": 11.24,
      "text": "Most astronomers believed a white dwarf, the dense remnant core of a dead star, was a planetary graveyard.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Hubble image of the Ring Nebula (Messier 57).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, and C. Robert O\u2019Dell (Vanderbilt University)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hubble_image_of_the_Ring_Nebula_(Messier_57).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.0,
          "end": 6.967,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 6.967,
          "end": 11.233,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.24,
      "end": 17.735,
      "text": "But in 1999, Hubble spotted mystery chemical signatures around a distant white dwarf.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "View of Hubble after Being Released from the Shuttle Atlantis (28223588012).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Hubble Space Telescope",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Hubble_after_Being_Released_from_the_Shuttle_Atlantis_(28223588012).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.233,
          "end": 13.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 13.8,
          "end": 17.733,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 17.735,
      "end": 23.655,
      "text": "Recently, researcher Jamie Williams used new data to solve this cosmic cold case.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Flickr via Openverse",
        "title": "Looking along Academic Loop Road towards the Degree Apprenticeship Centre",
        "credit": "chris.rycroft",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://www.flickr.com/photos/193316968@N06/52014624204"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 17.733,
          "end": 23.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 23.655,
      "end": 30.2,
      "text": "The mystery signatures turned out to be niobium, a heavy metal forged only during stellar death throes.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Element Origins.jpg",
        "credit": "JENNIFER JOHNSON",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Element_Origins.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 23.667,
          "end": 30.2,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.2,
      "end": 39.225,
      "text": "NASA's TESS telescope then detected a Jupiter-sized candidate planet orbiting the dead star every 4.4 days.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite artist concept (transparent background).png",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transiting_Exoplanet_Survey_Satellite_artist_concept_(transparent_background).png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.2,
          "end": 39.233,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 39.225,
      "end": 47.645,
      "text": "The white dwarf's intense radiation is stripping this second-generation planet's atmosphere, raining those heavy metals back down.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Hot Jupiter (35268999232).png",
        "credit": "Kevin M. Gill",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hot_Jupiter_(35268999232).png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 39.233,
          "end": 42.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 42.6,
          "end": 47.633,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 47.645,
      "end": 52.57,
      "text": "This cosmic recycling shows why dead stars are actually building new ones.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of the WDJ0914+1914 system.tif",
        "credit": "ESO/M. Kornmesser",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_WDJ0914%2B1914_system.tif"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 47.633,
          "end": 52.933,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
